audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Here''s a question that sounds simple: when people disagree, is there a fair way to turn everyone''s rankings into one choice for the group?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10200
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle
    '|b||Easy. Majority rule. Whatever most people prefer, the group prefers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5560
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:cu9upm'
    '|a||Let''s test that. A town of three equal groups must build one thing: a pool, a park or a library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8240
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1pnkam4'
    '|a||Group one ranks pool, then park, then library. Group two: park, library, pool. Group three: library, pool, park.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1gu7dhs'
    '|a||Now vote in pairs. Pool against park: groups one and three put the pool higher, so it wins two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1kec55f'
    '|b||Park against library: which wins? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4000
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:shn6jk'
    '|b||The park, two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1760
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated
    '|a||The park, two to one: groups one and two both rank it above the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle
    '|b||So the pool beats the park, and the park beats the library. Then the pool must beat the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1kusdc8'
    '|a||That''s what logic says. But groups two and three both prefer the library, so it beats the pool, two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:14p6ppr'
    '|a||The marquis de Condorcet spotted this in 1785: every voter is perfectly consistent, and yet the majority goes round in a circle.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:ylv1ei'
    '|a||That circle is our thread: who it hands power to, why Kenneth Arrow proved no voting rule fully escapes it, and how much it should worry us.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:14z55zw'
    '|b||Round and round, then. So which one does the town actually build?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5280
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:tz8ld4'
    '|a||That depends on the order of the votes. Say the chair puts pool against park first: the pool wins, then meets the library, and loses.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9680
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:whdvql'
    '|b||Now you chair the meeting, and you want the park. Which option do you hold back for the final vote? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1iwgkct'
    '|b||Hold back the park: the library wins the first vote, and the park beats the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5240
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated
    '|a||Hold the park back. Pool against library first, the library wins, and then the park beats the library. Whatever comes in last wins.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9920
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle
    '|b||So whoever sets the order picks the winner, and every single vote along the way looks fair.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6200
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:awhrlp'
    '|a||Could a cleverer rule fix it? In 1951 a young economist, Kenneth Arrow, asked whether any rule at all could do the job fairly.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1m6tp75'
    '|a||He asked for very little. Any rankings are allowed. If everyone prefers pool to park, so does the group. And no dictator, whose ranking always wins.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:av51ty'
    '|a||The result must be a proper ranking, with no circles. And the verdict on pool against park can''t depend on where people put the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1dzkyh6'
    '|b||That''s the bare minimum. Any decent rule would pass.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5000
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:io690h'
    '|a||Arrow proved that with three or more options, no rule meets all five at once. It''s part of why he shared the 1972 Nobel prize.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:oyo47'
    '|b||What about points, then? Two for first place, one for second. No circles there.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:4euyl9'
    '|a||No circles, but take five voters: three rank pool, park, library; two rank park, library, pool. On points the park wins, seven to six.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:oziaf3'
    '|a||Now the library drops out. Nobody changes their mind about pool and park, yet the pool wins, three to two. Points give up independence.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1x96b8k'
    '|b||So that''s it: Arrow proved democracy is impossible.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4240
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:ymb6zo'
    '|a||That''s the strong reading. In 1982 William Riker argued that votes can''t reveal a will of the people; at best they let us throw rulers out.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11680
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:18w7jbz'
    '|a||But the theorem is narrower: no rule works for every possible pattern of rankings. It says nothing about how often the bad patterns turn up.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1kjflaw'
    '|a||Take the gloomiest model: every voter picks a ranking at random, like rolling a die.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1hclm8i'
    '|a||With three voters and three options, a circle turns up 12 times in 216, about 6 percent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1qjyma1'
    '|b||And with millions of voters, still three options? Set your guess.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5240
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1e8radd'
    '|a||Just under 9 percent. And with five options instead of three, about one in four.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6640
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle
    '|a||But real voters aren''t dice. Their views share patterns, and Michel Regenwetter and colleagues showed that pushes circles down; in surveys and ballots they found almost none.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:e1brjm'
    '|a||And when Gerry Mackie rechecked the famous cases of cycling in 2003, Riker''s among them, most of them didn''t hold up.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1ikpxvh'
    '|b||So what keeps real electorates out of the circle?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3280
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:148nw60'
    '|a||Often, the options line up on one scale. Say a small, medium or large budget, and each voter likes options less the further they are from a favourite.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10960
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1m8w4lm'
    '|a||That shape is the rescue. Duncan Black showed in 1948 that then there''s no circle: the voter in the middle, the median voter, is on the winning side of every pair.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:6gr8hk'
    '|b||And our town failed that test. Every option was someone''s last choice, so no order of the three gives everyone a single peak.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10320
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1eo1rgs'
    '|a||Other rules step outside rankings altogether. In score or approval voting you grade each option, so Arrow''s theorem doesn''t apply; but grades must mean the same to everyone, and tactical voting still pays.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1wi155f'
    '|b||And politicians trade votes: you back my bridge, I''ll back your hospital.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5720
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1yrr3cq'
    '|a||Log-rolling lets strong feelings count, which rankings can''t. But it''s no cure: vote trading is tied to circles too, and when everyone trades, everyone can end up worse off.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:1jushdu'
    '|b||I''d have guessed small committees are where circles really lurk.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3920
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:1r6s13u'
    '|a||On pure chance, no: three voters circle a little less often than millions. But in a big electorate shared views average out and circles fade; a small committee gets no such protection.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:oibs1s'
    '|a||And committees weigh issues with many sides at once. Richard McKelvey showed in 1976 that then majority rule can circle almost everywhere, so whoever sets the agenda can steer the vote nearly anywhere.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:oqgfbq'
    '|a||So Arrow doesn''t say democracy is impossible. He says no ranking rule is perfect: each gives up one of these, or steps outside rankings altogether.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:17ykxpm'
    '|b||So choose what to give up, on purpose, and keep an eye on whoever sets the order.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated #ctx:18yvhbu'
    '|a||A five-member board picks one of four sites by votes in pairs, and the chair sets the order. What should worry you most?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle #ctx:17h0eaa'
    '|a||The order: if the sites form a circle, the one brought in last wins, and with four options a circle is more likely than with three.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle
    '|b||So, a fair way to add up our rankings? Not a perfect one. But a good one, if we know what we gave up.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7720
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated
    '|b||Whatever you hold back meets the winner of the other two. Which option beats that winner?':
